{"name":"Maximum Mocha","hex":"#6b4a40","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"maximum","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The highest limit.","The greatest value of a set or other mathematical structure, especially the global maximum or a local maximum of a function."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["To the highest degree."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/maximum"},{"term":"mocha","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A coffee drink with chocolate syrup added, or a serving thereof.","A flavour that combines coffee and chocolate."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark brown colour, like that of mocha coffee."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mocha"}],"entries":[{"title":"Maximum and minimum","blocks":[{"text":"In mathematical analysis, the maximum and minimum of a function are, respectively, the greatest and least value taken by the function. Known generically as extrema, they may be defined either within a given range or on the entire domain of a function. Pierre de Fermat was one of the first mathematicians to propose a general technique, adequality, for finding the maxima and minima of functions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Extrema_example_original.svg/330px-Extrema_example_original.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maximum_and_minimum","wikidata":"Q845060"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Modell_einer_Peanoschen_Fl%C3%A4che_-Schilling_XLIX%2C_1-.jpg/500px-Modell_einer_Peanoschen_Fl%C3%A4che_-Schilling_XLIX%2C_1-.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Modell_einer_Peanoschen_Fl%C3%A4che_-Schilling_XLIX%2C_1-.jpg/500px-Modell_einer_Peanoschen_Fl%C3%A4che_-Schilling_XLIX%2C_1-.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_c